\subtree{
  \title{OxCaml 56-Function Suite & Modal Python Bridge}

  \p{
    Alongside the tokamak control tasks above, \strong{baux} ships a 56-function synthetic black-box optimization catalogue (\code{ocaml/lib/problem.ml}, \code{ocaml/lib/formulas.ml}) spanning 16 multimodal, 10 bowl, 5 plate, 5 valley, 3 ridge, and 17 standard Bayesian optimization test surfaces across fixed #{1\text{D--}6\text{D}} domains and 18 scalable #{D}-dimensional families evaluated at canonical #{d}, #{D = 16}, and #{D = 64}. Compiled with \code{OxCaml 5.2.0+ox}, every objective evaluates over unboxed \code{float#} scalars and pair-interleaved \code{float64x2#} ARM64 NEON + \code{SLEEF 3.9.0} vector lanes scheduled across work-stealing domains via \code{Parallel_scheduler} and capsule-isolated \code{Batch.workspace} blocks.
  }

  \<div>[id]{ocaml-bench-simulation}{}

  \p{
    To expose these kernels to Python without per-call reference-counting overhead or memory-safety hazards, \code{ocaml/pybridge/py_obj.mli} and \code{baux.benchmarks.oxcaml} implement a modal CPython C-API bridge following Trangez [[Tra26]](https://blog.janestreet.com/oxcaml-typesafe-reference-counting-python/). Borrowed Python inputs (\code{str} identifiers, coordinate vectors, and 2D C-contiguous \code{float64} \code{numpy.ndarray} views acquired via \code{PyObject_GetBuffer} in \code{Py_obj.F64_buffer.with_matrix}) enter OCaml as \code{Py_obj.t @ local} with \strong{zero} \code{Py_INCREF}/\code{Py_DECREF} traffic; OxCaml's stack-locality mode statically rejects stashing borrowed handles into heap references or escaping closures (\code{ocaml/tests/unsafe_py_escape.ml}). Newly allocated Python return values—including the #{N \times 8}-byte \code{bytearray} output buffer viewed zero-copy via \code{np.frombuffer}—carry \code{Py_obj.t @ unique} (\code{ob_refcnt = 1}), use \code{borrow_} to preserve uniqueness while populating list, dictionary, or buffer slots (\code{PyList_SetItem} steals \code{@ unique} items directly via \code{Py_obj.List.set_steal}), and transfer ownership to CPython on return with zero extra increments (\code{ocaml/tests/unsafe_py_decref.ml} statically rejects use-after-\code{decref}), while \code{Modes.Portable.t} caches resident \code{Parallel_scheduler} worker pools across calls.
  }

  \pre{
import numpy as np
import baux
from baux.benchmarks import oxcaml

bounds, objective = oxcaml.problem("ackley", dim=16, workers=4)
state = baux.init(bounds, baux.enn(k=10) | baux.ucb(beta=2.0), key=baux.key(0))
for _ in range(12):
    state, batch = baux.ask(state, num_arms=8)
    state, _ = baux.tell(state, batch, objective(batch.points))

grid = np.zeros((4096, 16), dtype=np.float64)
vals = oxcaml.eval_batch("ackley", grid, backend="simd", workers=4)
assert vals.shape == (4096,) and np.allclose(vals, 0.0, atol=1e-12)
  }

